fix(gmail-labeler): the sync path keeps readonly-only scopes; the labeler scope set is separate

Review round 1, two blockers:

1. TYPE BREAK: GMAIL_SCOPE had become a tuple, and two callers still
   wrapped it - ai/gmail.py:223 scopes=[GMAIL_SCOPE] (a list containing
   a tuple) and connectors/google/gmail.py:271 SCOPES=(GMAIL_SCOPE,)
   (a tuple of a tuple) - both on the poller/connector credential path.
2. SCOPE ESCALATION ON THE SYNC PATH: the live token holds
   gmail.readonly only; requesting gmail.modify at its refresh risks
   invalid_scope - mail ingest would break on deploy before the user
   re-consents.

Fix: GMAIL_SCOPE is back to the readonly STRING, untouched - the sync,
poller and connector credential path is byte-identical to main
(SCOPE_BY_CONNECTOR['email'] reverted too). The labeler gets a separate
GMAIL_LABEL_SCOPES = (readonly, gmail.modify), used ONLY by
build_labeler_client and the new explicit consent path
'brain gmail-label --auth' (the labeler's own subcommand). --apply
first REFRESHES the token once (stdlib POST to the token endpoint -
no google libs, no quota, no Gmail call) and inspects the GRANTED
scopes; a readonly-only token fails with the re-consent message and
ZERO Gmail calls (the check runs before reads too).

Red-first: (a) the real credential builder on the sync path receives
[readonly] exactly (asserted on the built Credentials, skipped where
the gmail extra is absent); (b) build_labeler_client requests
readonly+modify; (c) a readonly-only token + --apply -> exit 78, the
re-consent message, zero calls. The 3 scope-contract tests pin
readonly again. Full suite 1706 passed / 4 skipped.
